---
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const header = [
	[0, 1, 'kind', '0x11, video'],
	[1, 1, 'flags', 'bit 0 is keyframe'],
	[2, 1, 'SliceIndex', ''],
	[3, 1, 'SliceCount', ''],
	[4, 1, 'BlockIndex', ''],
	[5, 1, 'BlockCount', ''],
	[6, 1, 'ShardIndex', ''],
	[7, 1, 'DataShards', ''],
	[8, 1, 'ParityShards', ''],
	[9, 1, 'Epoch', ''],
	[10, 2, 'ShardLen', 'big-endian'],
	[12, 4, 'FrameID', 'big-endian'],
	[16, 4, 'BlockLen', 'big-endian'],
	[20, 8, 'CaptureTS', 'ns, host monotonic clock'],
	[28, 8, 'EncodeTS', 'ns, host monotonic clock'],
] as const;
const kinds = [
	['0x11', 'KindVideo', 'Video shard'],
	['0x12', 'KindAudio', 'Opus packet'],
	['0x13', 'KindInput', 'Keyboard, mouse and gamepad input'],
	['0x14', 'KindRumble', 'Gamepad rumble'],
	['0x15', 'KindPing', 'Clock offset probe'],
	['0x16', 'KindPong', 'Clock offset reply'],
	[
		'0x17',
		'KindFeedback',
		'Client’s per-frame report: arrival time and shard counts, the host’s loss signal',
	],
] as const;
---

<Docs slug="protocol">
	<h2 id="two-planes">Two planes</h2>
	<p>
		Planck splits a session into a control plane and a media plane. Control runs over one QUIC
		connection: pairing, negotiation, stats, key derivation and the clipboard, which gets its own QUIC
		stream. Media (video, audio, input, gamepad) runs over raw UDP datagrams sealed with AES-128-GCM.
		Tailscale's LocalAPI carries no traffic: it only supplies discovery, peer identity and path facts. The repo's transport spike chose this
		split: across 12 wired configurations, raw sealed UDP had a median network p50 of 1.28 ms against
		1.75 ms for quic-go datagrams, and quic-go's congestion control stalled on Wi-Fi in 2 of 12 runs
		(<code>docs/spikes/spike3-transport/RESULTS.md</code>).
	</p>
	<Table label="Default ports">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Port</th><th scope="col">Protocol</th><th scope="col">Carries</th><th scope="col">Host setting</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td class="num">41000</td><td>UDP</td><td>Discovery probes and beacons</td><td><code>discoveryPort</code>, <code>-discovery-port</code> (0 turns it off)</td></tr>
				<tr><td class="num">41001</td><td>UDP (QUIC)</td><td>Control</td><td><code>controlPort</code>, <code>-listen</code></td></tr>
				<tr><td class="num">41002</td><td>UDP</td><td>Media</td><td><code>mediaPort</code>, <code>-media-port</code> (0 picks a free port)</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<p>
		The ports sit below 47981 to 48500, the range Sunshine uses. A host refuses a config where the
		control and media ports are the same.
	</p>

	<h2 id="control">Control: QUIC</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>ALPN <code>planck-v1</code>, TLS 1.3 only. The control protocol is <code>wire.Version</code> 1.</li>
		<li>Every host and client has a self-signed ECDSA P-256 certificate. Trust is the SHA-256 fingerprint pinned at pairing, not a certificate authority.</li>
		<li>The client checks the host against its pinned fingerprint. The host checks the client through its <code>Authorize</code> callback: a paired fingerprint, a PIN proof, or Tailscale WhoIs.</li>
		<li>A PIN proof is <code>HMAC-SHA256(PIN, role byte ‖ TLS exporter "EXPORTER-planck-pair-v1", 32 bytes)</code>, so a proof only works inside the TLS session it was made for.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="media-keys">Media keys and sealing</h2>
	<p>
		The media keys come from the TLS 1.3 exporter of the control connection (RFC 8446 §7.5) with the label
		<code>EXPORTER-planck-media-v1</code>. The cipher suite byte is bound into the exporter context. The
		exporter yields one key and one 12-byte IV per direction, client to host and host to client, so the
		media keys belong to that one control session.
	</p>
	<Table label="Sealed datagram overhead">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col" class="num">Bytes</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Marker</td><td class="num">1</td><td>Cleartext</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Sequence number</td><td class="num">8</td><td>Cleartext, 64-bit big-endian; a sliding window rejects replays</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Payload</td><td class="num">n</td><td>AES-128-GCM ciphertext</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Tag</td><td class="num">16</td><td>GCM authentication tag</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Overhead</td><td class="num">25</td><td><code>AEADOverhead</code></td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<p>
		ChaCha20-Poly1305 is implemented in <code>pkg/transport</code> but the session never selects it.
	</p>

	<h2 id="datagrams">Datagram sizes</h2>
	<Table label="Datagram constants">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Constant</th><th scope="col" class="num">Value</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>wire.MaxDatagram</code></td><td class="num">1152<span class="u">B</span></td><td>Default. Fits Tailscale's 1280-byte tunnel MTU.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>wire.MaxLANDatagram</code></td><td class="num">1452<span class="u">B</span></td><td>LAN with a 1500-byte MTU, only when both ends agree (<code>FeatureLANDatagram</code>).</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>wire.VideoHeaderLen</code></td><td class="num">36<span class="u">B</span></td><td>Plaintext video shard header.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Video shard payload</td><td class="num">1091<span class="u">B</span></td><td>1152 − 25 (AEAD) − 36 (header).</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>wire.MaxSlices</code></td><td class="num">16</td><td>Slices per frame.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>wire.MaxBlocks</code></td><td class="num">32</td><td>Reed-Solomon blocks per slice.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>wire.MaxShards</code></td><td class="num">255</td><td>Data plus parity shards per block.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h3 id="media-kinds">Media kinds</h3>
	<Table label="Media datagram kinds">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Byte</th><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Carries</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				{kinds.map(([b, name, what]) => (
					<tr><td class="num">{b}</td><td><code>{name}</code></td><td>{what}</td></tr>
				))}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h3 id="video-header">Video header</h3>
	<p>
		Each video shard starts with this 36-byte header (<code>wire.VideoHeader</code>). The client maps
		<code>CaptureTS</code> and <code>EncodeTS</code> onto its own clock with the ping and pong offset.
	</p>
	<Table label="Video header layout">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col" class="num">Offset</th><th scope="col" class="num">Size</th><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				{header.map(([off, size, field, note]) => (
					<tr><td class="num">{off}</td><td class="num">{size}</td><td><code>{field}</code></td><td>{note}</td></tr>
				))}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h2 id="fec">Forward error correction</h2>
	<p>
		Each slice is protected with Reed-Solomon (<code>klauspost/reedsolomon</code>). A block holds at most
		128 data shards; a larger slice splits into up to 32 blocks.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Target:</strong> the smallest parity count <em>k</em> for which the chance of losing more than <em>k</em> shards in a block, at the smoothed loss estimate, is under 10<sup>−3</sup>. Parity is capped at 100% of data.</li>
		<li><strong>Tracking:</strong> loss is averaged over a 2,000-shard window and tracked rising four times faster than falling. Below 0.1% loss a path counts as clean, and the first loss on a clean path sets the estimate at once.</li>
		<li><strong>Floor:</strong> <code>FECMinLoss</code> keeps some parity on clean paths, and keyframes always carry at least one parity shard.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		In the transport spike (240 fps, 100 Mbps, wired LAN), 1% loss with no FEC delivered 66 to 70% of
		frames; 20% parity delivered all of them. At 5% loss small per-slice blocks needed about 40% parity,
		and the adaptive policy chose 39.6% and delivered 99.8 to 99.9%. On a clean link it used 2.6 to 10%.
	</p>

	<h2 id="rate-control">Rate control</h2>
	<p>
		Planck runs its own delay-based controller (<code>pkg/ratectl</code>), close to Google Congestion
		Control: a trendline over one-way delay, plus a loss-based cut only above 10% loss, at most once per
		300 ms or one RTT. It starts at 20 Mbps with a 1 Mbps floor.
	</p>
	<Table label="Bitrate ceilings by path">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Path</th><th scope="col">How it is detected</th><th scope="col" class="num">Ceiling</th><th scope="col">Where</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>LAN</td><td>Loopback, private or link-local address</td><td class="num">300<span class="u">Mbps</span></td><td><code>ratectl.MaxKbps</code>, <code>defaults</code> LAN ceiling</td></tr>
				<tr><td>LAN, discovery hint</td><td>Host found by LAN discovery</td><td class="num">150<span class="u">Mbps</span></td><td><code>discovery.LANMaxBitrateKbps</code>: the most spike 3 sustained on wired LAN</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Tailscale direct</td><td><code>100.64.0.0/10</code> or <code>fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48</code></td><td class="num">100<span class="u">Mbps</span></td><td><code>tailscale.DirectMaxBitrateKbps</code></td></tr>
				<tr><td>Tailscale relay (DERP)</td><td>Tailscale reports a relayed path</td><td class="num">20<span class="u">Mbps</span></td><td><code>tailscale.RelayMaxBitrateKbps</code>, <code>defaults</code> WAN rate</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<Callout kind="caveat">
		The repo does not trace which of the two LAN ceilings binds in a live session. <code>ratectl</code>
		also has an 8 Mbps relay cap, but no code path classifies a link as a relay, so it is never used:
		the relay figure in effect is 20 Mbps. Host and client both default to a 100 Mbps maximum
		(<code>maxBitrateKbps</code>).
	</Callout>
	<p>
		On the LAN, video goes out in one burst per frame; there is no video pacer on that path. On Windows,
		timestamps and pacing use <code>QueryPerformanceCounter</code>, because Go's monotonic clock advances
		in about 0.5 ms steps on the test host.
	</p>

	<h2 id="audio">Audio</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>48 kHz stereo, encoded with Opus (<code>thesyncim/gopus</code>, pure Go): 10 ms frames by default, 20 ms allowed, 128 kbps.</li>
		<li>Playback through <code>ebitengine/oto/v3</code> with a 20 ms device buffer and 20 ms read-ahead.</li>
		<li>The host captures with ScreenCaptureKit on macOS and WASAPI loopback on Windows. The microphone goes the other way, in mono, to a virtual input device on the host.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="input">Input and gamepad</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Input rides the media plane as <code>KindInput</code>. The host drops duplicate sequence numbers; for 2 s after a frame loss, the client resends the previous event.</li>
		<li>Windows hosts inject keys by US-layout scan code (the physical key position), not virtual-key code, with <code>SendInput</code>. macOS hosts post CGEvents.</li>
		<li>Gamepads become virtual pads through ViGEmBus on Windows; rumble returns as <code>KindRumble</code>.</li>
		<li><code>planck-host -no-input</code> ignores keyboard, mouse and gamepad from clients.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="clipboard">Clipboard</h2>
	<p>
		The clipboard syncs <code>both</code> ways by default; <code>host-to-client</code>,
		<code>client-to-host</code> and <code>off</code> are the other settings. Windows carries
		<code>CF_UNICODETEXT</code>, PNG and <code>CF_DIB</code>; macOS carries plain text, PNG and TIFF. Items
		go over a one-way QUIC stream on the control connection, not the media plane, so a large image never
		holds up video or input.
	</p>

	<h2 id="tailscale">Tailscale LocalAPI</h2>
	<p>
		Planck talks to the local Tailscale daemon through its LocalAPI rather than embedding a tailnet node.
		LocalAPI is for discovery, identity and path metadata only; control and media go over the tailnet as
		ordinary QUIC and UDP.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Discovery:</strong> clients probe tailnet addresses as well as the LAN.</li>
		<li><strong>Identity:</strong> WhoIs reports whether a peer belongs to the host's own Tailscale user, which lets it pair with no PIN.</li>
		<li><strong>Path:</strong> the path info reports direct or relayed, the MTU, and a recommended bitrate of 100 Mbps direct or 20 Mbps relayed.</li>
		<li><strong>Sizing:</strong> Tailscale's tunnel MTU is 1280 bytes, which is why the default datagram is 1152. Over a tailnet each media packet costs 60 bytes more on the wire than over the LAN.</li>
	</ul>
</Docs>
